From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 15:53:21 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] webOS: rename group DMs and take people out of them, and tell
 group DM events

* Rename a group DM from the Messaging app

The Messaging app can now rename a group (imlibpurpleservice's
renameGroup), which the transport hands to the prpl as the
"webos-im-group-rename" signal (account, chat, name). Take it for a
group DM the user is in and PATCH /channels/{id} with the new name, as
a new group DM is named once it is made; return TRUE when the request
went out. Return FALSE for another account, for a chat that is not a
group DM (a server channel is the server's to name), and for a name
that is empty once its white space is trimmed.

Discord takes a group DM name of 1-100 characters (the Group DM params
of Modify Channel in discord-api-docs). A longer one is not sent: the
rename returns FALSE and says so in the group as a notice line. When
Discord refuses a rename, its message ("Missing Access", ...) goes in
the group as a notice and in the debug log, which never carries the
name.

Discord's answer is the renamed channel; its name is taken at once
through discord_set_channel_name, as the gateway's CHANNEL_UPDATE echo
does: the blist chat's "name" component and the conversation title
change, and the transport reads the new name off the chat with the next
line it stores for the group.

tests/test_group_rename.c builds the rename part of libdiscord.c,
marked by BEGIN and END lines, and checks what is sent, what is refused
and the notices.

* Take someone out of a group DM from the Messaging app

The Messaging app can now remove people from a group
(imlibpurpleservice's removeFromGroup), which the transport hands to
the prpl as the "webos-im-group-remove" signal (account, chat,
members), the members being buddy names joined by "\x1f" as for
"webos-im-group-invite". Take it for a group DM the user is in and
DELETE /channels/{id}/recipients/{user} for each member the plug-in
knows, the reverse of the PUT discord_group_dm_add_recipient makes;
return TRUE when at least one request went out. Return FALSE for
another account, for a chat that is not a group DM (a server channel's
members are the server's) and when no member is known. An unknown
member is skipped, and so is the user: leaving a group is
"webos-im-group-leave".

Discord's documentation (Group DM Remove Recipient in
discord-api-docs) says no more than "Removes a recipient from a Group
DM", nothing about who may. When Discord refuses, its message
("Missing Permissions", ...) goes in the group as a notice line and in
the debug log. Done, Discord answers with no body and the gateway's
CHANNEL_RECIPIENT_REMOVE takes the member off the group, as it does
when someone else removes them.

tests/test_group_rename.c also builds the new part of libdiscord.c,
between its BEGIN and END group remove lines, and checks the requests
made per member, what is not taken, and the notices.

* Open a group DM for its members without its history

imlibpurpleservice's getChatMembers (Group Info) reads a group's people
off its libpurple conversation, and a group DM has one only once a
message in it arrived after the transport started. Its join_chat cannot
be used to open it: discord_join_chat fetches the channel's messages
each time, sets "chan-backfilled-<id>" and can write a pins line.

Take the transport's "webos-im-chat-open" signal (account, chat),
which it emits for a chat with no conversation, for a group DM the
user is in: open the conversation alone, as discord_open_chat makes it
(the "id" data, the title), and put its people on it, the user as its
nick, as discord_got_channel_info does. They come from the recipients
READY gave; when one of them is not known, or none are kept, from GET
/channels/{id}, of whose answer only "recipients" is taken. No message
is fetched, nothing is acked or subscribed, no line is written and the
backfilled marker is left alone, so opening the group in the app later
still fetches its history. Return TRUE when the conversation is open
(now or already), FALSE for another account or a chat that is not a
group DM.

tests/test_group_rename.c also builds the new part of libdiscord.c,
between its BEGIN and END chat open lines, and checks what is opened,
what is asked of Discord, and that nothing else is done.

* Say in a group DM who renamed it, added or removed someone

Discord sends a group DM's rename as a message of type
MESSAGE_CHANNEL_NAME_CHANGE (4) whose content is the new name, and
someone added or removed as MESSAGE_RECIPIENT_ADD (1) / _REMOVE (2)
with no content and the person in mentions[0]. discord_process_message
wrote the rename as an ordinary message saying only the new name (an
outgoing one when the user renamed it) and skipped the other two, whose
content is empty, so the Messaging app showed nothing for them.

Write these three in a group DM as a system line with no sender
instead, as tdlib-purple writes its chat notices
(serv_got_chat_in with who " " and PURPLE_MESSAGE_SYSTEM), which
imlibpurpleservice stores with no notification:
  <name> changed the group name to "<new name>" / You changed ...
  <name> added <name> / You added <name> / <name> added you
  <name> removed <name> / You removed <name> / <name> removed you
  <name> left the group / You left the group
The names come from discord_get_display_name_or_unk, already escaped,
and the new name is escaped with purple_markup_escape_text. The line
carries its message id (webos-msg-id), so the transport keeps it once
across restarts, and goes through the plug-in's dedup by message id
first (now discord_webos_message_seen), so a history replay does not
repeat it; with the group not open it is not written and its id is
forgotten, as for any message, so the group's history writes it.

The gateway's CHANNEL_RECIPIENT_ADD/REMOVE handling is left as it is:
it updates the people on the conversation, and the "entered/left the
room" lines libpurple writes for it are PURPLE_MESSAGE_SYSTEM without
PURPLE_MESSAGE_RECV, which incoming_message_cb in imlibpurpleservice
drops. The message is what is kept: it has an id, and history brings it
for a change made while signed off.

That drop also took the notices discord_webos_group_notice wrote with
purple_conversation_write_system_message (a rename or a removal Discord
refused): they are now written the same way as these lines.

tests/test_group_rename.c also builds the new part of libdiscord.c,
between its BEGIN and END group event lines lines, and checks a rename
by someone else and by the user, adding, removing and leaving, a replay
of the same message and a group that is not open.

* Write a group DM's add, remove and rename lines at once

On a Sargo the lines for someone added to or removed from a Discord
group DM came late, or not at all. A message in a group DM with no
conversation goes through discord_join_chat_by_id before it is
written, which opens the conversation and fetches the group's history,
and the message returns there when a fetch went out. Its id was already
marked written, so the history's copy of it was skipped as a repeat and
the line came only with a later fetch, if ever.

Take a group DM's event message (MESSAGE_CHANNEL_NAME_CHANGE,
MESSAGE_RECIPIENT_ADD/_REMOVE) before that: when the group has no
conversation, open it for its people alone, the way "webos-im-chat-open"
does (now discord_webos_open_group_dm: no history, nothing acked or
subscribed, "chan-backfilled-<id>" untouched), and write the line at
once. The id dedup stays, so the history's copy writes nothing more.
Only when libpurple makes no conversation is the id forgotten, so the
history writes it later.

The gateway's CHANNEL_RECIPIENT_ADD/REMOVE now also keeps the group
DM's recipients, which READY gave and nothing updated, so a group
opened afterwards has the people in it now; it no longer hands libpurple
a NULL conversation when the group is not open. It still writes no line
the app keeps: who added or removed someone is only in Discord's
message for it.

tests/test_group_rename.c checks an event in a group that is not open
(opened with its people, the line written at once, its replay not
written), one with someone not known (only the channel asked of
Discord), libpurple making no conversation, and the recipients kept.

* Do not crash when someone is added to an open group DM

Adding someone to a Discord group DM from the Messaging app crashed
imlibpurpleservice when the group's conversation was open. Discord
answers with a CHANNEL_RECIPIENT_ADD on the gateway, and its handler
asks discord_get_user_flags for the new member's flags with the
channel's guild. A group DM has no guild, so that is NULL, and
discord_get_user_flags read guild->id before looking at it.

A user's flags in a chat with no guild are now only the bot flag, which
is what a group DM can show. Taking someone out goes through
purple_chat_conversation_remove_user and never asked for flags.

tests/test_user_flags.c checks a group DM (no guild) for a person and a
bot, and a server's roles: none, kick or ban (half-op), admin (op), a
role the server does not know, and a membership in another server.

* Say in a group DM why someone could not be added

Adding someone to a Discord group DM from the Messaging app could fail
with nothing to show for it. Two paths were silent:

- the person was not one the plug-in knows (a friend, or someone from a
  shared server or chat): webos-im-group-invite and chat_invite only
  wrote a debug line and went on;
- Discord refused the PUT /channels/{id}/recipients/{user}: the request
  had no callback, so its answer was dropped. Discord refuses anyone who
  is not a friend with error 50007.

Both now write a line in the group, as a rename or remove that fails
does. One the plug-in does not know: "Could not add <who>: Discord does
not know them here. Only your Discord friends can be added to a group."
A refusal with 50007: "Could not add <who>: Discord lets you add only
your friends to a group."; any other in Discord's own message, or with
its code when it gave none. <who> is the person as the user picked them,
HTML-escaped as the chat's lines are; the debug log has the code and
message but not the person.

Looking people up through the friends list as well would not help: the
friends are already among the users the plug-in knows, under the same
name as their buddy name, which is the app's address for them: the
username, with a #discriminator only for an old-style name.

tests/test_group_rename.c builds the new group add part and checks
adding, an unknown person alone and among known ones, a 50007, another
refusal, an escaped name and message, a code or a message alone, and a refusal
after signing off.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [webOS specific]
